  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY NIGHT THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 300 PM MDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\nAfter a potent, multi-day storm event, the end of the week and into \nthe start of next week, will be quiet with a return to warmer and \ndrier weather. Once the low pressure system responsible for the \nample mid-week moisture moves east out of the area, upper-level \nridging will take its place. The ridge will usher in warmer air \naloft, allowing surface temperatures to rise. Subsidence under the \nridge will keep cloud cover away, helping to amplify high \ntemperatures during the day. On Friday, temperatures will rebound \nback to seasonable, with highs reaching the 50s and 60s. By \nSaturday, temperatures will be back above average as 700 mb \ntemperatures reach the 90th percentile of NAEFS climatology. Highs \neast of the Laramie Range will reach the 70s, leading to a warm fall \nday.\n\nThe ridge will flatten out briefly on Sunday as a shortwave passes \nsouth through the Four Corners region. Impacts from this shortwave \nwill be minimal, with high temperatures only falling a few degrees \ncompared to Saturday. Expect a breezier day on Sunday as well with \nstrong west winds in the typical southeast Wyoming wind prones. Warm \nand dry conditions continue once again into the start of the work \nweek. Temperatures will remain above average for Monday and Tuesday \nwith dry conditions expected both days. Precipitation chances begin \nto increase Tuesday night as a deep trough pushes into western\nCONUS.",
